e: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.05746v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://arxiv.org/html/2609.05746v1: (400) Content length is too large: 7186949\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2609.05655] Tame polynomial automorphisms (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.05655)\nciteturn312view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.05655\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Algebraic Geometry\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 4 Sep 2026]\nL9: # Title:Tame polynomial automorphisms\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Stéphane Lamy L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Tame polynomial automorphisms, by St\\'ephane Lamy\nL14: \nL15: cite6†View PDF cite7†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:The group of polynomial automorphisms of the affine n-space is an interesting large group. A slightly simpler group is its subgroup of tame automorphisms. Natural problems about these groups include the existence of normal subgroups, the classification of finite subgroups, the Tits alternative, and the possible dynamical degrees of their elements.\nL17: One method to investigate these questions is via some actions on some metric spaces, namely the coset complex and the valuation complex, that we introduce in detail. This paper is a survey focusing on the following three cases: dimension 2, dimension 3, and dimension 4 for tame automomorphism preserving a nondegenerate quadratic form.\nL18: Subjects:  | Algebraic Geometry (math.AG); Geometric Topology (math.GT)\nL19: Cite as:  | cite8†arXiv:2609.05655 [math.AG]\nL20:    | (or cite9†arXiv:2609.05655v1 [math.AG] for this 